**Proof.**

Write $a_i = \prod_p p^{v_p(a_i)}$ where $v_p(a_i)\ge 0$ is the $p$-adic valuation (only finitely many are nonzero). For a pair $(j,k)$ with $j<k$, note that $a_j\nmid a_k$ iff there exists a prime $p$ with $v_p(a_j)>v_p(a_k)$. The replacement sends
$$v_p(a_j),\,v_p(a_k)\;\longmapsto\;\min(v_p(a_j),v_p(a_k)),\;\max(v_p(a_j),v_p(a_k))$$
for every prime $p$ simultaneously. In particular the *multiset* $\{v_p(a_j),v_p(a_k)\}$ is unchanged, so for each prime $p$ the multiset $\{v_p(a_1),\dots,v_p(a_n)\}$ is **invariant** throughout the whole process.

**Termination.** Define
$$\Phi \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} i\,\Omega(a_i),\qquad \Omega(m)=\sum_p v_p(m).$$
The total $\sum_i\Omega(a_i)=\sum_p\sum_i v_p(a_i)$ is invariant (the per-prime multiset is fixed). Consider one move on $(j,k)$. For a fixed prime $p$ with $x=v_p(a_j),\,y=v_p(a_k)$, the contribution of positions $j,k$ to $\sum_i i\,v_p(a_i)$ changes from $jx+ky$ to $j\min(x,y)+k\max(x,y)$. If $x\le y$ this is unchanged; if $x>y$ it changes by
$$j\,y+k\,x-j\,x-k\,y=(k-j)(x-y)>0.$$
Since the move is performed only when $a_j\nmid a_k$, there is at least one prime with $x>y$, so $\Phi$ **strictly increases** (by an integer $\ge 1$). On the other hand $\Phi$ is bounded above: with the per-prime multisets fixed, $\sum_i i\,v_p(a_i)$ is maximized when the valuations of $p$ are arranged in nondecreasing order, so
$$\Phi\le \Phi_{\max}:=\sum_p\sum_{i=1}^n i\,\bigl(\text{$i$-th smallest value among }\{v_p(a_1),\dots,v_p(a_n)\}\bigr)<\infty.$$
Being an integer that strictly increases and is bounded above, $\Phi$ can change only finitely many times. Hence the process terminates.

**Uniqueness of the final sequence.** When the process stops, for every $j<k$ we have $a_j\mid a_k$; in particular
$$a_1\mid a_2\mid\cdots\mid a_n,$$
so for every prime $p$,
$$v_p(a_1)\le v_p(a_2)\le\cdots\le v_p(a_n).$$
Thus at termination the list $(v_p(a_1),\dots,v_p(a_n))$ is the **sorted** (nondecreasing) arrangement of the invariant multiset $\{v_p(a_1),\dots,v_p(a_n)\}$, which is determined solely by the initial sequence. Therefore each $v_p(a_i)$ in the final sequence is uniquely determined, and so
$$a_i^{\text{final}}=\prod_p p^{\,(\text{$i$-th smallest of the invariant multiset of }v_p)}$$
is independent of the choices made during the process. $\blacksquare$

